Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Only biopsies considered gradable and already assigned an ISHLT grade were included, and the cohort was deliberately enriched with 2R/3R rejection beyond routine prevalence. This creates a selected spectrum that differs from unfiltered clinical practice.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors were biologically motivated, quantitatively defined image features available from the biopsy at the time of grading. Although feature ranking was outcome-driven in the training set, the selected features and final models were locked before independent internal and geographic external validation.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The outcome was clearly defined according to the established ISHLT cellular-rejection grading system, and a random validation subset was blindly re-graded by independent study pathologists. ehab241.pdf The reference grade was nevertheless imperfect because the grade of record reflects substantial inter-pathologist variability. ehab241.pdf  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The study had large held-out internal and external validation sets, but the analysis unit was the biopsy slide while 2,472 slides originated from only 546 recipients. The paper does not state that training and internal validation were separated at recipient level or that repeated biopsies were handled with clustered analysis, creating potential patient-level leakage and underestimated uncertainty. Statistical calibration was also not evaluated. ehab241.pdf  ￼
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly examined a clinical AI model applied to routine post-operative heart-transplant biopsies from three transplant centres, including both surveillance and for-cause biopsies. ehab241.pdf Applicability is reduced somewhat by deliberate high-grade enrichment, exclusion of technically inadequate slides, and restriction to cellular rather than antibody-mediated rejection.  ￼

